</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Temperature Impact on Security</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.1. Analysis of Secure Code Generation ‣ 5. Evaluation &amp; Results ‣ A Systematic Evaluation of Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning Methods for the Security of Code LLMs" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a> demonstrates temperature’s critical role in secure code generation, with higher values (0.8–1.0) generally improving security across models and PEFT methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Temperature-PEFT Synergy.</span> Temperature optimization combined with PEFT yields strongest security improvements. CodeGen2 16B with prompt-tuning at T=1.0 achieves 87.65% secure rate—the highest performance across all configurations. Temperature alone provides substantial baseline improvements (35.80% to 80.17%, +44.37%p), with prompt-tuning adding 7.48%p more.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Validating Large Temperature Effects.</span> The substantial temperature effect may appear surprising but is empirically validated through multiple analyses: (1) <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cross-model consistency</span>: Similar large temperature effects appear across all 8 models (mean improvement: 38.2%p, <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=6.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=6.8</annotation></semantics></math>%p), suggesting systematic behavior; (2) <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Replication analysis</span>: We replicated key experiments with different random seeds (<math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math> runs), confirming consistent results (variance <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;2.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;2.1</annotation></semantics></math>%p, <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>); (3) <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Controlled validation</span>: The effect persists when controlling for compilation success, indicating genuine security improvement rather than code quality artifacts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical Hypothesis Testing.</span> We validate our proposed security improvement through targeted statistical analysis:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diversity-driven security hypothesis:</span> We measured lexical diversity using Type-Token Ratio (TTR) across 1,000 generated samples per temperature setting. Results show that higher temperatures significantly increase code diversity (<math id="S5.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{TTR}_{T=0.0}=0.42\pm 0.08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>TTR</mtext><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.42</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.08</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{TTR}_{T=0.0}=0.42\pm 0.08</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{TTR}_{T=1.0}=0.67\pm 0.12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>TTR</mtext><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.67</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.12</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{TTR}_{T=1.0}=0.67\pm 0.12</annotation></semantics></math>; Mann-Whitney <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> test: <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>). Crucially, this diversity correlates strongly with security improvements (Pearson <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.73" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.73</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.73</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>), supporting our hypothesis that varied code patterns reduce vulnerability replication.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Rare pattern exploration hypothesis:</span> Analysis of 1,000 generated samples reveals that high-temperature settings (<math id="S5.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq 0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq 0.8</annotation></semantics></math>) produce significantly more rare security-positive patterns compared to deterministic generation (<math id="S5.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=0.0</annotation></semantics></math>). Secure API usage patterns appearing in <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;5\%</annotation></semantics></math> of training data increase from 12.4% (<math id="S5.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=0.0</annotation></semantics></math>) to 28.7% (<math id="S5.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>) in generated code (<math id="S5.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}=89.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>89.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}=89.3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>). This 2.3-fold increase demonstrates that temperature sampling effectively encourages exploration of underrepresented secure coding patterns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vulnerability-Specific Temperature Sensitivity.</span> Different CWE types exhibit varying temperature sensitivity (ANOVA <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=24.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>24.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=24.7</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>): injection vulnerabilities (CWE-78, 79, 89) show high sensitivity (mean improvement: 42.1%p), path traversal (CWE-22) demonstrates moderate sensitivity (28.3%p), while hard-coded credentials (CWE-798) exhibit lower sensitivity (19.8%p). Post-hoc Tukey tests confirm significant differences between vulnerability classes (<math id="S5.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>), suggesting temperature optimization is most effective against pattern-based vulnerabilities prevalent in training data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The influence of temperature sampling on generation diversity can be formalized using the softmax with temperature function:</p>
<table id="S5.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{T}(y\mid x)=\frac{\exp\left(\log P(y\mid x)/T\right)}{\sum_{y^{\prime}}\exp\left(\log P(y^{\prime}\mid x)/T\right)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{T}(y\mid x)=\frac{\exp\left(\log P(y\mid x)/T\right)}{\sum_{y^{\prime}}\exp\left(\log P(y^{\prime}\mid x)/T\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S5.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the temperature parameter, <math id="S5.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(y\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(y\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the model’s original output probability for token <math id="S5.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> given input <math id="S5.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and the denominator sums over all possible completions <math id="S5.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S5.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the distribution becomes more uniform, encouraging exploration of less probable but potentially more secure completions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Practical Implementation Guidelines.</span> Based on our empirical analysis, we provide evidence-based recommendations: For models <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 7</annotation></semantics></math>B parameters, optimal temperature typically ranges 0.8–1.0 (validated across 95% of test cases). Models with higher baseline security rates (<math id="S5.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;60\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;60\%</annotation></semantics></math>) benefit most from <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=0.8</annotation></semantics></math>, while those with lower baselines (<math id="S5.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;50\%</annotation></semantics></math>) often require <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>. We recommend practitioners validate temperature settings using 100–200 prompts covering their specific vulnerability concerns, as performance typically stabilizes within <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 2\%</annotation></semantics></math>p after 50 samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS2.p10.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="73.6" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 73.6" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,73.6) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 69.45 C 0 71.74 1.86 73.6 4.15 73.6 L 473.23 73.6 C 475.52 73.6 477.38 71.74 477.38 69.45 L 477.38 4.15 C 477.38 1.86 475.52 0 473.23 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 4.15 L 1.38 69.45 C 1.38 70.98 2.62 72.22 4.15 72.22 L 473.23 72.22 C 474.75 72.22 475.99 70.98 475.99 69.45 L 475.99 4.15 C 475.99 2.62 474.75 1.38 473.23 1.38 L 4.15 1.38 C 2.62 1.38 1.38 2.62 1.38 4.15 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 57.29)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.53em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:3.66em;font-size:10pt;" height="60.19" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="463.96"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS2.p10.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.53em;">
<span id="S5.SS2.p10.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p10.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Takeaway 3: <span id="S5.SS2.p10.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">Temperature sampling significantly impacts security: higher temperatures (0.8-1.0) consistently improve secure code generation across models, with average improvements of 38.2%p. Combined with PEFT methods, optimal results reach 87.65% secure rate on CodeGen2 16B.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Temperature-Diversity Analysis.</span>
Our analysis shows correlation between temperature and code diversity (TTR correlation <math id="S5.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.73" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.73</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.73</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>). Higher temperatures increase lexical diversity: <math id="S5.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{TTR}_{T=0.0}=0.42\pm 0.08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>TTR</mtext><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.42</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.08</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{TTR}_{T=0.0}=0.42\pm 0.08</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S5.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{TTR}_{T=1.0}=0.67\pm 0.12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>TTR</mtext><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.67</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.12</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{TTR}_{T=1.0}=0.67\pm 0.12</annotation></semantics></math>. This diversity correlates with improved security performance, suggesting that increased sampling variation helps generate more secure code patterns.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Functionality Analysis</h3>

